Done. Added to `Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift`:

```swift
public enum NoteParseError: Error, Equatable {
    case malformedFormat
}

public func parseNote(from text: String) throws -> Note {
    let parts = text.split(separator: "\n---\n")
    guard parts.count == 4 else {
        throw NoteParseError.malformedFormat
    }

    let pinnedString = parts[2].trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespacesAndNewlines)
    let pinned = pinnedString == "1"

    return Note(
        id: String(parts[0]),
        title: String(parts[1]),
        body: String(parts[3]),
        tags: extractTags(from: String(parts[3])),
        pinned: pinned
    )
}
```

What it does:
- Splits on the literal `"\n---\n"`, requiring exactly 4 parts — otherwise throws `NoteParseError.malformedFormat` (no guessing, no crash).
- Field order: `id`, `title`, `pinned` (a `"1"`/`"0"` flag → `Bool`), `body`.
- Tags come from the existing `extractTags(from:)` applied to the body.

Verification:
- `swiftc -parse` on the file passes.
- Standalone logic test confirms: valid 4-field input round-trips (id/title/pinned/tags/body), and 3-field / single-field inputs throw `malformedFormat`.

